"""`use lazy` is an optimization: it must never change what a program does.

The two lanes do not agree on whether it optimizes anything. The pure-Simple
interpreter really defers: `src/compiler/10.frontend/core/interpreter/eval_decls.spl`
answers a lazy `DECL_USE` with `register_deferred_module(...)`, and
`module_loader_core.try_force_any_deferred_for` materializes the module on the
first unresolved lookup of one of its names. The Rust bootstrap seed loads it
eagerly and says so
(`interpreter_module/module_evaluator/evaluation_helpers.rs:575`), so on the
deployed seed (sha256 3d120a6f9ab5704b..., aarch64) the keyword buys nothing:
an entry importing `app.io.cli_lint_commands` opened **1,109 `.spl` files with
`use lazy` and 1,109 with an eager `use`** -- byte-identical, reproduced on this
tree at f26970e9d93.
See doc/08_tracking/bug/use_lazy_is_a_no_op_in_the_rust_seed_2026-09-12.md

What both lanes must agree on, and what this spec pins, is the SEMANTICS: for
the same program, the lazy and eager spellings produce the same output and the
same exit code, whether or not the imported name is ever used. That is the
contract a future seed-side implementation of deferral must not break, and it is
what makes the divergence above a pure cost question rather than a behavioural
one. The spec deliberately does NOT pin the seed's open counts: freezing the
eager behaviour would turn the defect into a requirement.

Fails closed: a fixture run that produced no output is a FAIL, and an empty
source read is a FAIL. `SIMPLE_BINARY` selects the binary under test; it
defaults to `bin/simple`.
"""

use std.spec.*
use std.fs.{read_file}
use std.io_runtime.{process_run}

val FIXTURE_DIR: text = "test/01_unit/compiler/loader/lazy_use_equivalence"
val SIMPLE_EVAL_DECLS: text = "src/compiler/10.frontend/core/interpreter/eval_decls.spl"

fn run_sh(command: text) -> (text, i64):
    val result = process_run("sh", ["-c", command])
    (result.0 + result.1, result.2)

fn run_fixture(entry: text) -> text:
    """Program output plus a trailing `rc=<n>` line, so a differing exit code
    shows up in the same comparison as differing output."""
    val script = "b=$SIMPLE_BINARY; test -n \"$b\" || b=bin/simple; "
        + "timeout 300 $b run " + FIXTURE_DIR + "/" + entry + " 2>/dev/null; "
        + "echo \"rc=$?\""
    val (out, _code) = run_sh(script)
    out

describe "use lazy equivalence":
    it "gives the same result for both spellings when the import is used":
        step("run the eager spelling")
        val eager = run_fixture("used_eager.spl")
        expect(eager.len()).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(eager).to_contain("message=helper")
        step("run the lazy spelling")
        val lazy = run_fixture("used_lazy.spl")
        expect(lazy.len()).to_be_greater_than(0)
        step("output and exit code must be identical")
        expect(lazy).to_equal(eager)
        expect(lazy).to_contain("rc=0")

    it "gives the same result for both spellings when the import is never used":
        step("a deferred loader may skip the module entirely here")
        val eager = run_fixture("unused_eager.spl")
        expect(eager.len()).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(eager).to_contain("message=unused")
        val lazy = run_fixture("unused_lazy.spl")
        expect(lazy.len()).to_be_greater_than(0)
        expect(lazy).to_equal(eager)
        expect(lazy).to_contain("rc=0")

    it "keeps the pure-Simple lane's deferral path wired":
        step("read the interpreter's use-declaration arm")
        val source = read_file(SIMPLE_EVAL_DECLS)
        expect(source.len()).to_be_greater_than(0)
        step("a lazy use must register a deferred module instead of loading")
        expect(source).to_contain("decl_get_is_lazy(did)")
        expect(source).to_contain("register_deferred_module(module_path, current_file, imported_names)")
        step("and the eager path must still load immediately")
        expect(source).to_contain("load_module_selective(module_path, imported_names, current_file)")
